● Deep Learning

  ---
  What is Deep Learning?

  A subset of Machine Learning that uses Neural Networks with many layers to learn patterns from data automatically.

  Artificial Intelligence
          └── Machine Learning
                  └── Deep Learning  ← we are here

  ---
  Brain Inspiration

  Deep Learning is inspired by the human brain.

  Human Brain                    Deep Learning
  ───────────────────────────────────────────
  Neurons                   →    Nodes
  Synapses (connections)    →    Weights
  Learning from experience  →    Training
  Strong signal passes      →    Activation Function

  ---
  Simple Neuron

  Inputs        Weights        Neuron          Output
    x1 ──× w1 ──┐
    x2 ──× w2 ──┼──→ SUM ──→ Activation ──→  y
    x3 ──× w3 ──┘

  Step 1: Multiply each input by its weight
  Step 2: Sum all values + bias
  Step 3: Pass through activation function
  Step 4: Output result

  Mathematically:
  y = activation(w1×x1 + w2×x2 + w3×x3 + bias)

  ---
  Neural Network Structure

  Input Layer    Hidden Layers       Output Layer
     (x)         (learning)              (y)

    [x1]──→ [N] [N] [N]              [output]
    [x2]──→ [N] [N] [N] ──────────→ [output]
    [x3]──→ [N] [N] [N]              [output]

            Layer1 Layer2 Layer3

  ┌───────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │     Layer     │              Role               │
  ├───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Input layer   │ Receives raw data               │
  ├───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Hidden layers │ Learns patterns (the deep part) │
  ├───────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Output layer  │ Gives final prediction          │
  └───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

  Deep = many hidden layers (usually 3+)

  ---
  Activation Functions

  Controls whether a neuron fires or not.

  ReLU (most common)

  f(x) = max(0, x)

  x = -3 → output = 0   (neuron off)
  x =  5 → output = 5   (neuron on)

  Sigmoid

  f(x) = 1 / (1 + e^-x)

  Output always between 0 and 1
  Used for binary classification

  Softmax

  Converts outputs to probabilities that sum to 1
  Used for multi-class classification

  [2.0, 1.0, 0.5] → [0.62, 0.23, 0.15]

  ---
  How Deep Learning Learns

  Forward Pass

  Input → through all layers → prediction

  Calculate Loss

  Loss = difference between prediction and actual answer

  Predicted: 0.8   Actual: 1.0
  Loss = (1.0 - 0.8)² = 0.04

  Backward Pass (Backpropagation)

  Go backwards through network
  Adjust weights to reduce loss

  Repeat

  Do this thousands of times until loss is minimized

  ---
  Full Training Loop

  for each epoch:
      ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
      │  1. Forward pass  → get prediction  │
      │  2. Calculate loss                  │
      │  3. Backward pass → adjust weights  │
      │  4. Repeat with next batch          │
      └─────────────────────────────────────┘

  Loss gets smaller each time → model improves

  ---
  Types of Deep Learning Networks

  1. ANN — Artificial Neural Network

  Basic neural network
  Used for: tabular data, regression, classification
  Example: predicting house prices

  2. CNN — Convolutional Neural Network

  Specialized for images
  Detects edges → shapes → objects
  Used for: image recognition, medical imaging
  Example: detecting cats vs dogs

  3. RNN — Recurrent Neural Network

  Has memory — looks at previous inputs
  Used for: sequences, time series
  Example: predicting next word, stock prices

  4. LSTM — Long Short Term Memory

  Improved RNN — better long-term memory
  Used for: speech recognition, translation
  Example: Google Translate

  5. Transformer

  Attention mechanism — focuses on important parts
  Used for: NLP, large language models
  Example: ChatGPT, BERT, Claude

  ---
  Deep Learning vs Machine Learning

  ┌────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬───────────────┐
  │                            │       Machine Learning        │ Deep Learning │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ Feature extraction         │ Manual (human picks features) │ Automatic     │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ Data needed                │ Small to medium               │ Large         │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ Training time              │ Fast                          │ Slow          │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ Hardware                   │ CPU                           │ GPU           │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ Interpretability           │ Easier to explain             │ Black box     │
  ├────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────┤
  │ Performance on images/text │ Moderate                      │ Excellent     │
  └────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┴───────────────┘

  ---
  Real Life Applications

  ┌────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   Field    │             Application              │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Vision     │ Face recognition, self driving cars  │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ NLP        │ ChatGPT, Google Translate, Siri      │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Healthcare │ Cancer detection, drug discovery     │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Finance    │ Fraud detection, stock prediction    │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Gaming     │ AlphaGo, game bots                   │
  ├────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Audio      │ Speech recognition, music generation │
  └────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Key Hyperparameters

  Learning Rate  → how big each weight update step is
  Epochs         → how many times to train on full dataset
  Batch Size     → how many samples per weight update
  Layers         → how deep the network is
  Neurons        → how wide each layer is
  Dropout        → randomly turn off neurons to prevent overfitting

  ---
  Summary

  Deep Learning
    = Neural Networks + Many Layers + Large Data + GPU

  Process:
    Raw Data
      → Input Layer
      → Hidden Layers (automatically learn features)
      → Output Layer
      → Prediction

  Learns by:
    Forward pass → calculate loss → backprop → update weights
    Repeat until loss is minimized

  The more data and layers, the more complex patterns it can learn — from edges in images to understanding human language.


