● Form Smallest Number From Two Digit Arrays

  ---
  Problem Statement

  Given two arrays of single digits (1-9), find the smallest number that contains at least one digit from each array.

  ---
  Simple Example

  Array 1 = [4, 1, 3]
  Array 2 = [5, 7, 1]

  Answer = 1

  Because 1 appears in both arrays — it alone satisfies both conditions.

  ---
  Two Cases

  ---
  Case 1 — Common Digit Exists

  Array 1 = [4, 1, 3]
  Array 2 = [5, 7, 1]

  Common digits = {1}
  Smallest common digit = 1

  Answer = 1  (single digit number)

  A single digit that exists in both arrays is always smaller than any 2-digit number.

  ---
  Case 2 — No Common Digit

  Array 1 = [4, 1, 3]
  Array 2 = [5, 7, 2]

  Common digits = none

  Must form a 2-digit number using one digit from each array.

  Pick the smallest digit from each array:
  Smallest in Array 1 = 1
  Smallest in Array 2 = 2

  Form the smallest 2-digit number:
  Option A: put smaller digit first → 12
  Option B: put larger digit first  → 21

  Answer = 12  (always put smaller digit first)

  ---
  Why Single Digit Always Wins

  Smallest possible 2-digit number = 11
  Any single digit                 = 1 to 9

  Single digit 1 < any 2-digit number
  Single digit 9 < any 2-digit number (11)

  So if common digit exists → always use it

  ---
  Step by Step Approach

  Step 1: Find common digits between both arrays
             common = Array1 ∩ Array2

  Step 2: If common digits exist
             Answer = smallest digit in common

  Step 3: If no common digits
             a = smallest digit in Array 1
             b = smallest digit in Array 2
             Answer = smallest 2-digit combo of a and b
                    = put min(a,b) first, max(a,b) second

  ---
  More Examples

  Example 1 — Common digit

  Array 1 = [2, 5, 6]
  Array 2 = [5, 3, 9]

  Common = {5}
  Answer = 5

  Example 2 — No common digit

  Array 1 = [3, 5, 2]
  Array 2 = [8, 6, 9]

  No common digits
  Smallest in Array 1 = 2
  Smallest in Array 2 = 6

  Options: 26 or 62
  Answer  = 26

  Example 3 — Multiple common digits

  Array 1 = [1, 3, 5, 7]
  Array 2 = [3, 5, 9]

  Common = {3, 5}
  Smallest common = 3
  Answer = 3

  Example 4 — Same smallest digits

  Array 1 = [4, 6, 8]
  Array 2 = [4, 7, 9]

  Common = {4}
  Answer = 4

  ---
  Edge Cases

  ┌───────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │         Situation         │                     Handling                      │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Multiple common digits    │ Take the smallest one                             │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ One array has digit 1     │ If common → answer is 1, if not → 1X (very small) │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Both arrays share digit 1 │ Answer = 1 always                                 │
  ├───────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No overlap at all         │ Form 2-digit from min of each                     │
  └───────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  Visual Decision Tree

             START
               │
      ┌────────▼─────────┐
      │ Common digit      │
      │ exists?           │
      └────────┬──────────┘
               │
        ┌──────┴──────┐
       YES            NO
        │              │
        ▼              ▼
    Smallest       Pick min(A)
    common digit   and min(B)
        │              │
        ▼              ▼
     ANSWER        Put smaller
                   digit first
                       │
                       ▼
                    ANSWER

  ---
  Summary

  Two cases only:

  1. Common digit exists
     → answer = min(common digits)     ← single digit always best

  2. No common digit
     → a = min(Array1), b = min(Array2)
     → answer = min(a,b) × 10 + max(a,b)  ← smallest 2-digit combo

